.\" Manpage for tclrs.
.\" Contact https://github.com/MenkeTechnologies/tclrs to correct errors or omissions.
.TH TCLRS 1 "2026-07-28" "tclrs 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tclrs \- Tcl compiled to fusevm bytecode (a tclsh-compatible driver)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tclrs
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIFILE\fR [\fIarg\fR ...]
.br
.B tclrs
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\-c\fR \fISCRIPT\fR [\fIarg\fR ...]
.br
.B tclrs
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tclrs
parses Tcl and lowers it to
.B fusevm
bytecode \(em the execution engine shared with
.BR zshrs ,
.BR stryke ,
and the other fusevm frontends. There is no interpreter loop and no code
generator in tclrs itself: a script is compiled once to a
.B fusevm::Chunk
and executed by the VM, rather than re-parsed on every evaluation.
.PP
The reference implementation is
.BR tclsh (1)
9.0.4. It is the specification: behavior is ported from it, and the test suite
runs every program through both and compares the output byte for byte.
.PP
Every VM tclrs builds arms fusevm's three-tier Cranelift JIT. A hot loop inside
a procedure reaches a compiled trace; a loop at a script's top level does not,
because a top-level variable is a VM global.
.B \-\-tiers
reports which tiers a given script actually reached.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I FILE
Run the script in \fIFILE\fR. One script: the first failure ends it, the message
goes to standard error followed by \fB    (file "..." line N)\fR when the
failure was located while compiling, and the process exits 1.
.TP
.BR \-c " " \fISCRIPT\fR
Run \fISCRIPT\fR instead of a file. Arguments after it are the script's.
.TP
.BR \-\-aot " " \fIOUT\fR
Compile the script through fusevm's closed-world compiler and link it into a
standalone native executable at \fIOUT\fR \(em no parser and no bytecode
dispatch loop inside it. Refused for a script that uses
.B catch
or a coroutine, which need a driver outside \fBVM::run\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-aot\-object " " \fIOUT\fR
Emit the relocatable object only, to be linked against \fBlibtclrs.a\fR by hand.
.TP
.B \-\-lsp
Speak the Language Server Protocol on standard input and output: diagnostics,
completion, hover, signature help and document symbols, every answer drawn from
the compiler's own tables.
.TP
.B \-\-dap
Speak the Debug Adapter Protocol on standard input and output: breakpoints,
stepping, stack frame, variables, and the program's output as events. The
script is compiled with a line marker before every command; an ordinary run
carries none.
.TP
.B \-\-tiers
Run the script, then report which fusevm tiers took its chunk.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-tokens
Print the parser's lexical output \(em one line per word and per part of a word,
with the shape (bare, braced, quoted, expand) that decides whether the part will
be substituted \(em and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-ast
Print the parse tree \(em script, command, word, part, with a command
substitution nested inside the word containing it \(em and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-disasm
Print the compiled fusevm bytecode instead of running it.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
An unknown option is refused rather than treated as a file name, which is the
one place this binary deliberately differs from
.BR tclsh (1).
.SH STANDARD INPUT
With no \fIFILE\fR and no \fB\-c\fR, the script is read from standard input.
.PP
Not a terminal: a sequence of commands, each evaluated as it completes. A
failure is reported and the next command still runs, and end of input exits 0
\(em which is why \fBtclrs < script\fR exits 0 where \fBtclrs script\fR exits 1.
Nothing is printed that the script did not print.
.PP
A terminal: the same evaluation behind a line editor \(em prompt, history in
\fB~/.tclrs/history\fR, completion of command names, ensemble subcommands and
variables on Tab, and multi-line editing decided by the parser rather than by
counting braces. Procedures defined at the prompt outlive the line that defined
them. \fBexit\fR, \fBexit N\fR, \fBquit\fR or Ctrl\-D leaves.
.SH LANGUAGE
Commands:
.BR set ", " unset ", " eval ", " puts ", " expr ", " incr ", " append ", "
.BR format ", " if ", " while ", " for ", " foreach ", " switch ", " break ", "
.BR continue ", " proc ", " return ", " global ", " catch ", " error ", "
.BR array ", " dict ", " coroutine ", " yield ", " yieldto ", " info coroutine ,
the
.B string
ensemble, and the list commands
.RB ( list ", " llength ", " lindex ", " lappend ", " lrange ", " lreverse ", "
.BR linsert ", " lreplace ", " lsearch ", " lsort ", " join ", " split ", "
.BR concat ).
.PP
All twelve parsing rules: braces, quotes, backslash substitution, command
substitution, variable and array-element substitution, comments, and \fB{*}\fR
expansion.
.PP
What is not built yet is refused with a message naming the construct, never
silently misread.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TCLRS_JIT
\fBoff\fR, \fB0\fR or \fBno\fR skips arming the JIT.
.TP
.B TCLRS_REPL_MODE
\fBvi\fR selects modal editing in the REPL; \fBemacs\fR is the default. Also
settable as \fBmode\fR under \fB[repl]\fR in \fB~/.tclrs/config.toml\fR.
.TP
.BR FUSEVM_JIT_BLOCK_THRESHOLD ", " FUSEVM_JIT_TRACE_THRESHOLD ", " FUSEVM_JIT_CACHE_DIR
fusevm's own knobs, unchanged.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.tclrs/history
REPL history, 5,000 commands.
.TP
.B ~/.tclrs/config.toml
REPL configuration.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the script ran to its end, 1 when a script failed, could not be read, or
an option was not understood.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a one-liner:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tclrs \-c 'puts [expr {1 + 2}]'      \(rh 3
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what the parser made of a word:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tclrs \-\-dump\-ast \-c 'puts "x is $x"'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compile a script to a native executable:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tclrs \-\-aot ./demo demo.tcl && ./demo
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tclsh (1).
.PP
